comments.body:
I'm afraid I have to complain as well, sorry. If I got you right [~rbarnes] the chosen solution won't change the "no clean & explicit history" problem, since the subject (that is still as it was before) is what is shown in "git log --graph --oneline" or SourceTree. When looking at our develop/release/master (however you call it) branch, we want to name the reason that causing the change an not that a brach named xyz was merged to our develop/release/master branch because of PR number #123.

To be clear we want a history like this:

{noformat}
* FOO-123 Awesome thing that has an expressive title to be clear what it is, while keeping it on one line
* FOO-124 Another thing that has an expressive title to be clear what it is, while keeping it on one line
{noformat} 

Instead of this:

{noformat}
* Merge pull request #41 in PROJECT/NotImportantSinceWeCanGetThisInfoElsewhere from feature/FOO-123-Awsome-thing-that-has-a
* Merge pull request #42 in PROJECT/NotImportantSinceWeCanGetThisInfoElsewhere from feature/FOO-124-Another-thing-that-has-a
{noformat}

The easiest way (not the most convenient way) would be to be able to change the subject when merging the PR. However a more convenient solution would be to just take the PR title as the subject for the merge commit.

Of course having something smart in place to configure how the merge commit message for a repo should look like (like suggested in the comments of STASH-2894) would be worth a thought. For example having a configuration like "$pull-request-title \n$list-of-reviewers\n$pull-request-description". However, this nice to have, the stuff above is important.
